+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — sled 1.0.0-alpha.124
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2,
+added as a dev-dependency). All tests live in `tests/test_tree.rs`, module
+`hegel_props`, alongside the existing quickcheck suite they supplement.
+
+All databases are opened via `Config::tmp()`, which places each Db in its own
+self-deleting temporary directory (the `Db` holds the `Config`, which holds the
+tempdir deleter, so teardown order is safe). The tests use `Db<3>` — the same
+tiny leaf fanout the existing quickcheck suite uses — so trees grow deep and
+split/merge constantly even with ~150 keys.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+1. **`hegel_tree_matches_btreemap_model`** — stateful model test (state
+   machine): a `Db<3>` must agree with a `BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, Vec<u8>>` under an
+   arbitrary interleaving of `insert`, `remove`, `get`, `compare_and_swap`,
+   `get_lt`, `get_gt`, `apply_batch`, `pop_first`, `pop_last`, `len`/`is_empty`/
+   `contains_key`, full forward/reverse iteration, and *restart* (drop the Db,
+   reopen from the same path). `first()`/`last()` are checked as an invariant
+   after every step. The configuration is generated too: flusher thread on/off,
+   zstd level from {-5, 0, 3, 22}, cache capacity from {0, 256, 1MiB}.
+   *Evidence*: this is a port (with broader generators — arbitrary byte values
+   including >4096-byte out-of-line values, plus batch/pop operations) of the
+   maintainers' own `prop_tree_matches_btreemap` quickcheck property in
+   `tests/tree/mod.rs`, which remains in place.
+
+2. **`hegel_apply_batch_matches_model`** — applying a `Batch` of staged
+   inserts/removes (with duplicate keys common, thanks to a tiny-keyspace key
+   generator) leaves the tree equal to the model with the same operations
+   applied sequentially; later writes to a key within a batch win.
+   *Evidence*: `apply_batch`/`Batch` doc comments in `src/tree.rs`.
+
+3. **`hegel_range_matches_model`** — `Tree::range` over arbitrary
+   `(Bound<Vec<u8>>, Bound<Vec<u8>>)` pairs (Included/Excluded/Unbounded in all
+   combinations, including inverted and empty ranges) yields exactly the model
+   entries satisfying the bounds, in order, both `collect()`ed forward and via
+   `.rev()`. *Evidence*: `range` accepts arbitrary `RangeBounds` per its
+   signature; `Iter` implements `DoubleEndedIterator`.
+
+4. **`hegel_double_ended_iteration_matches_model`** — interleaving `next()` and
+   `next_back()` on a single `Iter` per a drawn direction schedule must yield
+   each element exactly once (the `DoubleEndedIterator` contract). **This
+   property fails — real bug, kept as a deterministically-failing pinned test.**
+   See Suspected bugs below.
+
+5. **`hegel_compare_and_swap_semantics`** — `compare_and_swap(k, old, new)`
+   succeeds iff the current value equals `old` (both as `Option`); on success
+   `previous_value`/`new_value` are reported correctly and the key holds `new`
+   afterwards; on failure the error carries the actual current/proposed values
+   and the tree is unchanged. *Evidence*: `compare_and_swap` doc comment and
+   `CompareAndSwapError`/`CompareAndSwapSuccess` field docs in `src/lib.rs`.
+
+6. **`hegel_reopen_preserves_contents`** — after arbitrary inserts (including
+   values above `max_inline_value_threshold` = 4096, which take the separate
+   blob path) and removes, an optional explicit `flush()`, drop, and reopen
+   from the same path, the reopened tree contains exactly the model contents.
+   *Evidence*: `Db::drop` flushes when no flusher thread is configured
+   (`src/db.rs`); durability is the crate's headline promise.
+
+7. **`hegel_scan_prefix_matches_model`** — `scan_prefix(p)` yields exactly the
+   model entries whose keys start with `p`, forward and reversed. Keys are
+   built as prefix ++ suffix so probes match, and prefixes include all-`0xFF`
+   runs to exercise the "no successor exists" branch of `scan_prefix`'s upper
+   bound computation (`src/tree.rs`). *Evidence*: `scan_prefix` doc example.
+
+8. **`hegel_pop_in_range_drains_range_in_order`** — repeatedly calling
+   `pop_first_in_range`/`pop_last_in_range` (drawn schedule) over an arbitrary
+   bounds pair pops exactly the in-range entries in two-ended sorted order,
+   returns `None` when the range is empty, and leaves out-of-range entries
+   untouched. The documented panic case (`end == Excluded(K::MIN)`) is excluded
+   per contract. *Evidence*: `pop_{first,last}_in_range` doc comments/examples.
+
+9. **`hegel_checksum_is_history_independent`** — two trees with identical final
+   contents report equal `checksum()` (and `len()`), regardless of operation
+   history (different insertion order, overwritten placeholder values,
+   inserted-then-removed junk keys). *Evidence*: `checksum` doc: "Returns the
+   CRC32 of all keys and values", i.e. a pure function of contents.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Build: sled 1.0.0-alpha.124 compiles cleanly on rustc 1.95.0 (edition 2024);
+  hegeltest 0.28.2 added with a plain `cargo add --dev hegeltest`.
+- Default run (`cargo test --test test_tree hegel_props`): **8 of 9 properties
+  pass; property 4 fails with a real bug** (below) — `8 passed; 1 failed` in
+  194 s. The failure is deterministically pinned: its generators are boosted
+  toward the failing region (tiny non-empty trees, mixed-direction schedules),
+  and it fails and shrinks to the minimal counterexample in ~11 s on every run.
+- Liveness check: temporarily inverting the checksum assertion produced an
+  immediate shrunk counterexample (`n = 0`, both checksums 0); reverted.
+- Exploratory high-count run: the seven non-stateful passing properties were
+  each run once at `test_cases = 1000` (10x default) — all passed. The
+  stateful model test is heavily I/O-bound (each case opens, flushes, restarts
+  a real database; ~2 s/case), so its exploratory pass used
+  `test_cases = 300` (3x default) — passed in 270 s with no failures. All
+  counts were then reverted to the CI-tuned default for the committed tests.
+- The pre-existing quickcheck suite (`quickcheck_tree_matches_btreemap`) was
+  left untouched and still passes.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### `Iter` violates the `DoubleEndedIterator` contract: front and back cursors cross and re-yield elements
+
+- **Found by**: `hegel_double_ended_iteration_matches_model` (kept failing,
+  marked `KNOWN FAILURE` in `tests/test_tree.rs`).
+- **Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel): a tree containing the single
+  key `[]`; call `next_back()` (yields `([], [])`), then `next()` — it yields
+  `([], [])` *again* instead of `None`.
+- **Larger deterministic reproducer** (any leaf fanout, verified at both 3 and
+  1024):
+
+  ```rust
+  let db: sled::Db<3> = sled::Config::tmp()?.open()?;
+  db.insert(&[][..], vec![])?;
+  db.insert(&[1][..], vec![])?;
+  db.insert(&[250][..], vec![])?;
+  let mut iter = db.iter();
+  iter.next_back(); // Some(([250], []))
+  iter.next();      // Some(([], []))
+  iter.next_back(); // Some(([1], []))
+  iter.next();      // Some(([1], []))   <- duplicate
+  iter.next();      // Some(([250], [])) <- duplicate
+  ```
+
+- **Code path**: `src/tree.rs` — `impl Iterator for Iter` (`fn next`, around
+  line 2044) tracks its position in `next_fetch` + `prefetched`;
+  `impl DoubleEndedIterator for Iter` (`fn next_back`, around line 2087)
+  tracks its position in `next_back_last_lo` + `prefetched_back`. Neither end
+  ever consults the other's cursor, so once `next()`'s search key passes
+  `next_back()`'s low-water mark the same keys are served from both ends.
+  The std `DoubleEndedIterator` docs require that "both back and forth work on
+  the same range, and do not cross".
+- **Blast radius**: any user code that mixes `next()` and `next_back()` on one
+  `iter()`/`range()`/`scan_prefix()` iterator (e.g. `iter.next_back()` to peek
+  the max then continuing forward) silently sees duplicated entries.
+  Single-direction uses (`collect`, `.rev()`, `get_lt`, `get_gt`, `first`,
+  `last`, `pop_*`) are unaffected — which is why the other eight properties
+  pass.
+- Not fixed here per task instructions; the pinned test documents it.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- None abandoned outright. Two scoping decisions worth recording:
+  - `pop_{first,last}_in_range` with `end == Bound::Excluded(&[])` is excluded
+    by the generator because the method *documents* a panic for
+    `Excluded(K::MIN)` — a contract exclusion, not a workaround.
+  - The checksum property only asserts equality for equal contents; asserting
+    *inequality* for different contents would be unsound (CRC32 collisions are
+    legitimate).
+- Merge operators and transactions were listed as candidate angles in the task
+  but do not exist in this 1.0-alpha rewrite (both are commented out in the
+  source/tests), so no properties were written for them.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout: the suite compiled first try,
+  including the `#[hegel::state_machine]` machine, `one_of!` with `.boxed()`
+  branches, and helper-fns-taking-`&TestCase`. The "resource-owning machines
+  and Drop order" and "two live handles contend on a file lock" warnings mapped
+  exactly onto sled (`db: Option<Db>` declared before `config`, and take/None
+  before reopen in the restart rule).
+  - The "draw a bounded schedule up front and cycle it" pattern from the
+  streaming-API section was exactly right for double-ended iteration and
+  range-pop scheduling — and the schedule shrank beautifully (`[false, true]`).
+- The default-run shrink of the original broad double-ended failure hit the
+  300-second shrink cap (each test case opens a real database, and the
+  counterexample first appeared in a 150-key tree). The skill's advice to pin
+  known failures by *boosting the generator toward the failing region* worked
+  perfectly: the pinned version finds and fully shrinks the same bug in ~11 s.
+  A note in SKILL.md that heavyweight-fixture tests (one DB per case) shrink
+  slowly and benefit from re-running (the failure DB resumes shrinking) might
+  save others time.
+- One paper cut: there is no global case-count override (documented honestly in
+  the reference), so the 10x exploratory run required temporarily sed-editing
+  `test_cases` into nine attributes and reverting.
